 `pemu_x64` was validated against native runs of static and dynamic glibc programs, bash, python3,
 perl, gcc and common coreutils (on an x86_64 host); the aarch64-host build is the same code using the
 generic syscall ABI, but has not been executed on a real aarch64 machine or on Android/Termux.
-`pemu_x64` is an interpreter (no JIT): expect it to be 50-1000x slower than native. Arithmetic flags are evaluated lazily, which makes it a little faster, but it is still far slower than `qemu-user` (a JIT).
+`pemu_x64` without its JIT (`PEMU_JIT=0`, or when the JIT cannot be built/enabled) is a plain interpreter: expect it to be 50-1000x slower than native.
+
+`pemu_x64` (x86_64 guest on an aarch64 host) has a block-translating **JIT** too, on by default on aarch64 hosts. Guest basic blocks are compiled to AArch64 code and chained, indirect branches go through an inline cache, and a compare followed by `jcc`/`setcc`/`cmov` uses the host flags directly (`cmp` + `b.cond`); the lazy flag state of the interpreter is only written when the flags can still be observed. Native: moves (all sizes, `fs:` and rip-relative operands, `movzx/movsx/movsxd`, `lea`), `push/pop`, `call/ret/jmp/jcc` (direct and indirect), add/or/and/sub/xor/cmp/test, `neg/not`, shifts and `imul` when their flags are not needed, `setcc/cmovcc`, `cdq` family, and SSE data moves, `pxor/por/pand/pandn`, `movd/movq`. Everything else (x87, most SSE, string instructions, `div`, `adc/sbb`, `inc/dec`, ...) runs through the interpreter from inside the translated block, so behaviour is identical. Faults inside translated code report the exact guest `rip` and flags; pending signals are polled on backward and indirect branches. The cache is flushed when translated pages are unmapped, re-protected or `MAP_FIXED`-overwritten; pages that are writable *and* executable are never translated (x86 needs no explicit flush for self-modifying code), they are interpreted. The JIT switches itself off when the guest creates a second thread (the cache is not shared safely between threads yet) and falls back to the interpreter. Disable it with `PEMU_JIT=0` or `pemu -nojit`; `PEMU_JIT_STATS=1` prints block counts and the most frequent interpreter fallbacks at exit. If the JIT does not compile on your toolchain, `prx build` retries with `-DPEMU_NO_JIT`.
+
+**Testing the JIT without an aarch64 machine.** `make pemu-x64-jit` (x86_64 host) builds `pemu_x64` with `-DPEMU_JIT_EMBED`, linked with `pemu.c` built as an *AArch64 interpreter*: the JIT emits real AArch64 machine code and that code is executed by `pemu.c`, so the actual back end is exercised (slowly). `make check` runs the x86 self-tests, plus `tests/pemu_x64_fuzz.py`, which generates random x86-64 programs (ALU at all sizes, moves, shifts, `imul`, `setcc/cmov`, push/pop, loops, calls, SSE, `fs:`/rip-relative/SIB operands) and compares registers, flags and memory between the interpreter and the JIT. Not covered by that setup, and therefore **not yet verified on real hardware**: instruction-cache maintenance (`__builtin___clear_cache`), writable+executable anonymous mappings (SELinux/Android policy), and the real signal context (`uc_mcontext.pc`). Report problems with `PEMU_JIT=0` as the workaround.
 
 `pemu` (aarch64 guest on an x86_64 host) has a block-translating **JIT** that is on by default: guest basic blocks are compiled to x86_64 code, direct branches are chained, indirect branches go through an inline cache, and x86 flags are used directly (a compare followed by a branch/`csel` costs one `cmp` + `jcc`). Integer ALU, bitfield, multiply/divide, conditional select/compare, branches, scalar and vector (`ldr q`, `ldp q`) loads/stores are translated natively; everything else (FP arithmetic, NEON ALU, exclusives, ...) runs through the interpreter from inside the translated block, so behaviour is identical. Roughly 20x faster than the interpreter on integer / memory loops. The code cache is flushed when the guest unmaps, re-protects or `MAP_FIXED`-overwrites translated pages, and on `ic` instructions, so self-modifying code and `dlopen` work. Disable it with `PEMU_JIT=0` or `pemu -nojit` (the JIT also switches itself off if an executable mapping can't be created). `tests/pemu_fuzz.py` runs random programs under both engines and compares all registers, NZCV and memory.
 
